    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d128">

                <pb facs="0377" n="357" sameAs="frus:frus1946v02/pg357"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">C.F.M. Files: Lot
                    M–88: Box 2061: CFM Documents</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Communication by the Secretary General</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">La Grandville</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Council of Foreign
                            Ministers</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        C.F.M.(46) 66</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-11">11 May, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Southern Tyrol: Views of the
                        Austrian Government</hi></p>
                <p>I have the honour to forward herewith to the members of the Council copy of a
                    letter dated 10th May 1946, addressed to me by the Representative of the Federal
                    Government of Austria in Paris.</p>
                <p>Two copies of a map<anchor n="11" xml:id="d128-f1"/> which was attached to the
                    above letter will be forwarded to each Delegation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">La Grandville</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d128-a1.xml" xpointer="d128-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d128-f1" n="11" xml:id="d128-f1-content">
                <p>The map under reference is not reproduced here. Austrian territorial claims in
                    the South Tyrol are depicted on the map facing <ref target="#pg358">p.
                    358</ref>. The solid red line defines the earlier Austrian claim to the entire
                    South Tyrol. The broken red line defines the minor frontier rectifications
                    described in the document printed here.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
